---
// Privacy policy, English page (/datenschutz.html; the German page is
// /de/datenschutz.html, same slug so the language switcher pairs them).
// Describes FACTUALLY what the website does, checked section by section
// against the code on 29.10.2026. Keep it paragraph for paragraph identical
// in substance to src/pages/de/datenschutz.astro — the head comment there
// lists what it covers and what is still open with the operator.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
import Attribution from '../components/Attribution.astro';
import { IS_STAGING } from '../lib/seo';
---
<Layout title={"Privacy Policy | VerseBase"} description={"Privacy policy of VerseBase: hosting and Cloudflare, cookieless reach measurement with one-click opt-out, external images, YouTube only after a click, the feedback form, user accounts with Discord, profiles, friends and online status, and donations via Stripe and PayPal."} themeColor="#05070d" translated={true}>
<style is:inline>
:root{--bg:#05070d;--bg-2:#0a0e18;--surface:#0f1524;--surface-2:#141c30;--accent:#2dd4ff;--accent-2:#6ea8ff;--text:#e8eefc;--muted:#8a99b8;--line:rgba(110,168,255,.16);--line-soft:rgba(232,238,252,.10);--ink:#05070d;--maxw:840px;}
/* ⚠⚠ GESEHEN 30.08.2026 im Kontaktbogen: diese Seite rendert komplett in
   TIMES NEW ROMAN — Ueberschrift, Fliesstext, alles. Nachgemessen:
   getComputedStyle(body).fontFamily = "Times New Roman" auf
   /impressum, /datenschutz und beiden DE-Fassungen.
   Ursache: die Schrift-Token stehen NICHT global, sondern in den
   Seiten-Stylesheets (archive.css, data-page.css, account-dossier.css)
   bzw. bei eigenstaendigen Seiten in deren eigenem :root — siehe
   src/pages/404.astro Zeile 21. Diese Seite laedt keins davon und setzte
   sie auch nicht selbst, also lief jedes var(--font-…) ins Leere und
   der Browser nahm seinen Standard. Die Farbtokens kamen aus theme.css,
   deshalb sah die Seite auf den ersten Blick richtig aus.
   Dieselbe Zeile wie in 404.astro, damit beide dasselbe meinen. */
:root{--font-display:'Orbitron',sans-serif;--font-ui:'Rajdhani',sans-serif;--font-body:'Barlow',system-ui,sans-serif;--font-mono:'Share Tech Mono',monospace}
body{font-family:var(--font-body)}
/* Hellmodus — erzeugt von scripts/build-light-palettes.mjs. Nicht von Hand ändern. */
:root[data-theme="light"]{--bg:#f4f7fe;--bg-2:#e9eef9;--surface:#fdfdff;--surface-2:#eef2fc;--text:#1b1f29;--muted:#4e586c;--accent:#05657c;--accent-2:#0850ad;--ink:#ffffff;--accent-media:#2dd4ff;--accent-2-media:#6ea8ff;--line:rgba(43,125,148,.3);--line-soft:rgba(41,46,56,.12)}




/* ⚠ Gemessen 30.08.2026 (Tiefenpruefung): 3,2rem Polsterung = 57,6 px, die
   feste Kopfleiste ist aber 61 px (Schreibtisch), 69 px (Telefon) und
   86 px bei 768 px Breite hoch — mit Zurück-Zeile. Die Überschrift stand
   dadurch bei 768×1024 zwanzig Pixel HINTER der Leiste. --nav-h veröffentlicht
   SiteNav; der Rückfallwert deckt den Fall ohne SiteNav ab. */
.lg-wrap{max-width:var(--maxw);margin:0 auto;padding:calc(var(--nav-h, 69px) + 2.4rem) 1.25rem 4rem}
/* ⚠ GEMESSEN 31.08.2026: „Datenschutzerklärung" braucht bei dieser Größe
   456 px und hatte auf einem 360-px-Gerät 299 — der Browser brach mitten
   im Wort um („Datenschutze / rklärung"). Silbentrennung statt harter
   Schnitt: die Seite trägt lang="de", der Browser kennt die deutschen
   Trennmuster und setzt „Datenschutz- / erklärung". Die englische Fassung
   („Privacy Policy") ist kurz genug und bleibt unberührt — aber die Regel
   steht in beiden Sprachfassungen, damit sie nicht auseinanderlaufen. */
.lg-wrap h1{font-family:var(--font-display);font-weight:900;font-size:clamp(2rem,6vw,3rem);color:var(--text);margin:0 0 1.6rem;hyphens:auto;-webkit-hyphens:auto}
.lg-wrap h2{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:var(--ls-10);text-transform:uppercase;font-size:var(--fs-9);color:var(--accent);margin:2.2rem 0 .6rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);padding-bottom:.4rem}
.lg-wrap p, .lg-wrap li{color:var(--text);line-height:1.7;font-size:var(--fs-10)}
.lg-wrap ul{padding-left:1.2rem}
.lg-wrap .muted{color:var(--muted)}
.lg-wrap .optout{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:.75rem;background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:1rem;margin:1.1rem 0;scroll-margin-top:6rem}
.lg-wrap .optout button{font:inherit;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;color:var(--ink);background:var(--accent);border:0;border-radius:6px;padding:.5rem .95rem}
.lg-wrap .optout button:hover{filter:brightness(1.08)}
.lg-wrap .optout .note{flex:1 1 100%;margin:0;color:var(--muted);font-size:var(--fs-7);line-height:1.55}
.lg-wrap .optout code{font-size:.85em;color:var(--accent-2)}
.lg-wrap code{font-size:.85em;color:var(--accent-2)}
.lg-fill{display:inline-block;background:color-mix(in srgb,#ff5b5b 14%,transparent);border:1px dashed #ff5b5b;border-radius:4px;padding:0 .45rem;color:#ffb4b4;font-weight:700}
/* Hell-Entsprechungen — erzeugt von scripts/build-light-overrides.mjs. Nicht von Hand ändern. */
:root[data-theme="light"] .lg-fill{color:#893c40}
</style>
<SiteNav crumbs={[{ label: 'Archive', href: '/archiv.html' }, { label: 'Privacy' }]} hasEn={true} />
<main id="main">
  <div class="lg-wrap">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>

    <h2>Controller</h2>
    <p>
      Krisztofer Milánkovits, Schlossstraße 6, 85411 Hohenkammer, Germany —
      e-mail: <a href="mailto:krysx141@gmail.com">krysx141@gmail.com</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Principle</h2>
    <p>
      VerseBase is a non-commercial fan project without advertising and without comment functions.
      The pages themselves are ready-made, static files. Alongside them run an account system with
      server functions (<a href="#accounts">Supabase</a>) for accounts, profiles, friends, messages
      and donations, our own statistics server (<a href="#umami">Umami</a>) and a
      <a href="#discord-server">Discord bot</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Even while you just read, <a href="#cloudflare">Cloudflare</a> and
      <a href="#hosting">our server</a> process your connection data, and two cookieless reach
      measurements count the visit; you can <a href="#opt-out">opt out</a> with one click. Some
      images come from <a href="#images">external providers</a>, and some pages read public data
      from the account system. Everything else happens only when you trigger it: when you start a
      <a href="#youtube">YouTube video</a>, send <a href="#feedback">feedback</a>, use a
      <a href="#accounts">user account</a> or <a href="#support">donate</a>. This website itself
      sets only one cookie, the one for your opt-out (two more on the
      <a href="#staging">test preview</a>); in addition, <a href="#cloudflare">Cloudflare</a> sets
      protection cookies to fend off automated access. Details follow below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="hosting">Server log files (hosting)</h2>
    <p>
      Our web server (nginx) runs on a server of Hetzner Online GmbH (Industriestr. 25,
      91710 Gunzenhausen, Germany) that we operate; Hetzner acts as our processor for this. The web
      server logs every request: the IP address of the delivering connection (usually a Cloudflare
      server) and the forwarding chain sent along, which may contain your IP address, plus the time,
      the requested address including any appended parameters, status code, amount of data
      transferred, referring address and browser identifier (user agent). The logs serve the
      operation and security of the website; we keep them for up to ten years. Legal basis: Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR (legitimate
      interest in secure operation).
    </p>

    <h2 id="cloudflare">Delivery and reach measurement (Cloudflare)</h2>
    <p>
      The site is delivered via Cloudflare (Cloudflare, Inc., 101 Townsend Street, San Francisco,
      CA 94107, USA). Cloudflare sits in front of the hosting as a proxy: every request to this
      website and its subdomains (such as stats.verse-base.com and staging.verse-base.com) passes
      through it, and connection data (IP address, time, requested address, user agent) is
      processed there for delivery and to fend off attacks. To fend off automated access,
      Cloudflare also adds a small, invisible check script to every page (loaded from
      <code>/cdn-cgi/challenge-platform/</code>) that checks technical signals in your browser;
      Cloudflare stores the result in its own cookie (<code>cf_clearance</code>). Cloudflare may
      also set other technically necessary cookies (for example <code>__cf_bm</code>, valid for 30
      minutes). This protects the site and is not part of reach measurement, so your
      <a href="#opt-out">opt-out</a> does not affect it. Legal basis for the check script and the
      cookies: Section 25 (2) no. 2 TDDDG (strictly necessary to deliver the requested page
      securely).
    </p>
    <p>
      Cloudflare additionally adds <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong> to the pages, a
      measurement script that records page views, visits and load times. It sets
      <strong>no cookies</strong>, uses no fingerprinting and does not follow you across other
      websites; from this measurement we only see aggregated figures, never individual profiles.
      Legal basis: Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR (legitimate interest in secure, fast delivery and in basic
      reach figures). Cloudflare also processes data in the USA and is certified under the EU-U.S.
      Data Privacy Framework. For details see the provider's privacy policy:
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener">cloudflare.com/privacypolicy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="umami">Self-hosted reach measurement (Umami)</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Umami</strong> also runs at stats.verse-base.com, open-source analytics on our own
      server at Hetzner. Recorded are the page visited (only the path, without appended parameters
      or fragments), the page title, the referrer, screen size and browser language, device type,
      browser and operating system, the country with region and city derived from the IP address,
      and times and time on page. <strong>No cookies are set and nothing is stored on your
      device.</strong> To tell visits apart, the server computes a checksum from the IP address, the
      browser identifier and a secret value that changes every month; the IP address itself is not
      stored. We keep the statistics data for up to ten years. The requests pass through Cloudflare
      like every request to this website (see above);
      no other third party receives the data. Legal basis: Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR (legitimate interest
      in reach figures). Your <a href="#opt-out">opt-out</a> stops this measurement too.
    </p>

    <div class="optout" id="opt-out" data-staging={IS_STAGING ? '1' : undefined}>
      <span id="rum-status">Checking …</span>
      <button type="button" id="rum-toggle">Don't count me</button>
      <p class="note">
        Your choice is remembered by the technical cookie <code>vb_noanalytics</code> in this
        browser and only for this address; its entire content is a 1 or a 0, and it lasts 400 days.
        It takes effect from the next page load and covers both services: the server then no longer
        allows the measurement scripts, so they never load at all. If you clear your cookies, you
        have to opt out here again. For administrator accounts the site sets the same cookie by
        itself after sign-in, unless you previously chose “Count me again” in this browser; this
        browser is then no longer counted from the following page load on, even after signing
        out. Legal basis for the cookie: Section 25 (2) no. 2 TDDDG.
      </p>
    </div>
    <script is:inline>
      (() => {
        const NAME = 'vb_noanalytics';
        const off = () => document.cookie.split('; ').some((c) => c === NAME + '=1');
        const status = document.getElementById('rum-status');
        const btn = document.getElementById('rum-toggle');
        if (!status || !btn) return;
        // The preview build loads no counter (empty CSP), cookie or not.
        const staging = document.getElementById('opt-out')?.dataset.staging === '1';
        const render = (hint) => {
          status.textContent = (staging
            ? 'You are not counted on the test preview, whether or not you have opted out; your choice here applies only to this address.'
            : off()
            ? 'You are not being counted here.'
            : 'You are currently being counted here.') + (hint ? ' ' + hint : '');
          btn.textContent = off() ? 'Count me again' : "Don't count me";
        };
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          document.cookie = (off()
            ? NAME + '=0'
            : NAME + '=1') + '; Max-Age=34560000; Path=/; SameSite=Lax; Secure';
          render('Applies from the next page load.');
        });
        render();
      })();
    </script>

    <h2 id="storage">Storage in your browser (localStorage, sessionStorage)</h2>
    <p>
      Tools and settings of this website store data <strong>locally in your browser</strong>:
      working state (crafting plan and “owned” marks, fleet, hangar loadouts, mining workbench,
      calculator inputs such as the Precision Jump route) and settings (display, performance mode,
      hangar options, whether you have already seen a hint). For the lifetime of a tab only
      (sessionStorage), small markers are added, such as the details for the back link to the
      previous page. While you are signed out, no script sends this content anywhere; you can delete
      it at any time via your browser settings. Legal basis: Section 25 (2) no. 2 TDDDG (strictly
      necessary for the function you use). Some tools also write their state into the address bar,
      for example a hangar loadout for sharing; if you load such an address, it appears in the
      <a href="#hosting">server log</a> like any other.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you are signed in, your browser also holds your session (access and refresh keys plus
      account details such as your e-mail address and, where applicable, the details from Discord),
      your account role together with your account ID for the lifetime of the tab (re-fetched from
      the server at most every five minutes), a copy of your profile and, once you have opened the
      account dashboard, your <a href="#rsi">RSI code</a>, even if you never verify a handle. The
      crafting planner and “owned” marks, the fleet and hangar
      loadouts then also store their contents in your <a href="#accounts">user account</a> so they
      are available on all devices, and this browser keeps a copy. Whatever you had created in this
      browser while signed out moves into the account at the first sync; the fleet becomes
      favorites there. Mining presets and refinery jobs live only in the account. When you sign out
      in the account dashboard or delete your account, the site removes the session, the account
      role, the profile copy, the RSI code and the account copies of the tools from this browser.
    </p>

    <h2 id="images">Externally embedded images</h2>
    <p>
      Some images are loaded from external sources as soon as they appear on the page. For
      technical reasons, the respective provider then receives your IP address and browser
      identifier and at most the fact that the request comes from verse-base.com:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>media.starcitizen.tools</strong> (Star Citizen Wiki) and
        <strong>cstone.space</strong>: most ship images, as main image and thumbnail on ship pages,
        in the ship list, in the fleet on pilot profiles and on the tiles of saved ships and loadouts
        in the account dashboard</li>
      <li><strong>api.fleetyards.net</strong> (FleetYards): paints on the ship pages and fallback
        images for ships without an image of their own; the requests may be redirected to
        FleetYards' storage</li>
      <li><strong>cdn.discordapp.com</strong> (Discord Inc., USA): profile pictures taken over from
        Discord, on pilot profiles, in the friends list and chat and in the dashboard (see
        <a href="#discord">Discord</a>)</li>
      <li><strong>trgjhmbnodoarnfmlcqx.supabase.co</strong> (Supabase, see
        <a href="#accounts">user account</a>): uploaded profile pictures and banners</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Custom image addresses that someone enters in a profile are only displayed from these
      providers. Legal basis: Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR (legitimate interest in showing ships and profiles
      with pictures without hosting every image ourselves).
    </p>

    <h2>Background and ship videos (local)</h2>
    <p>
      Some patch pages show short, muted video loops (trailer excerpts) as backgrounds. These files
      are served <strong>locally by this website</strong>; no third-party server is contacted. With
      reduced motion enabled (prefers-reduced-motion), in data-saver mode (where the browser reports
      it), in the website's light mode or with a window narrower than 1000 pixels, only the still
      image is shown. Some ship pages have a video of their own instead of a YouTube commercial; it
      loads, also locally, only when you choose the “Video” view.
    </p>

    <h2 id="youtube">YouTube videos (click to load)</h2>
    <p>
      On patch and topic pages, videos are embedded as a local preview image. Only your click on it
      loads the player from youtube-nocookie.com, and the video starts right away. On ship pages
      with an official commercial, you choose the “Video” view and then click “Play commercial”;
      this click loads the player interface from www.youtube.com and the player from
      youtube-nocookie.com, and the video plays muted in a loop. <strong>Before the click, no
      connection to Google is made.</strong> The click applies to this one video only; the next page
      load loads nothing again.
    </p>
    <p>
      With the click, Google (Google Ireland Limited, Gordon House, Barrow Street, Dublin 4,
      Ireland) receives your IP address, browser data, the origin verse-base.com and, where your
      browser sends them, existing YouTube cookies. The player loads further content from Google's
      servers and may store data in your browser. A transfer to Google LLC in the USA is possible;
      Google LLC is certified under the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework. The legal basis is your
      consent given by the click (Art. 6 (1) (a) GDPR, Section 25 (1) TDDDG).
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google’s privacy policy</a>
      applies. References such as “Watch on YouTube” and YouTube channels in profiles are ordinary
      links; they transmit nothing until you follow them.
    </p>

    <h2 id="feedback">Feedback form (Web3Forms)</h2>
    <p>
      On the <a href="/feedback.html">Feedback</a> page you can voluntarily send us a message.
      Delivery is handled by the service <strong>Web3Forms</strong>. When you submit, the following
      data is transmitted to Web3Forms and delivered from there by e-mail to our mailbox at Google
      (Gmail):
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>message text</strong> (required)</li>
      <li><strong>rating</strong> (1–5 stars, optional)</li>
      <li><strong>e-mail address</strong> (optional, only if you want a reply; it is set as the
        reply-to address)</li>
      <li>fixed form details (form key, subject, sender name, a field for spam protection)</li>
      <li>technically, your IP address, browser identifier and language setting, the origin
        verse-base.com and the time of submission</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Nothing from your user account is sent along, even when you are signed in. The legal basis is
      your consent given by actively submitting the form (Art. 6 (1) (a) GDPR). Providing your
      e-mail address is voluntary; without it we cannot reply. We use the data solely to process
      and answer your feedback and do not pass it on for other purposes. The e-mail stays in our
      mailbox until we delete it; on request we delete it right away.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Web3Forms is operated by Web3Creative, based in India, and uses service providers in the EU
      and the USA; according to its data processing agreement, transfers rely on the European
      Commission's standard contractual clauses. By its own account, Web3Forms keeps submissions for
      up to three years. Details:
      <a href="https://web3forms.com/privacy" rel="noopener">web3forms.com/privacy</a>. The mailbox
      is provided by Google Ireland Limited; a transfer to Google LLC (USA, certified under the
      EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework) is possible. The form sets no cookies and loads no external
      scripts; your browser only connects to Web3Forms upon submission. Without JavaScript the form
      is sent as an ordinary form, and you land on a confirmation page from Web3Forms. When opened,
      the Feedback page also loads the <a href="#testpilots">Test Pilots list</a> from the account
      system.
    </p>

    <h2 id="accounts">User account (Supabase)</h2>
    <p>
      You can voluntarily create a user account, with an e-mail address and password or with your
      <a href="#discord">Discord account</a>. Registration with an e-mail address is currently
      paused; new accounts can only be created with Discord, and existing accounts keep signing in
      with e-mail address and password. The account feature is provided via
      <strong>Supabase</strong>; the database is located in an EU data center (AWS Frankfurt,
      region eu-central-1). The following data is processed:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>e-mail address</strong> and <strong>password</strong> (the password is stored
        exclusively as a cryptographic hash, never in plain text). If you create the account with
        Discord, the e-mail address comes from Discord and there is no password at first. If you
        later set one in the account dashboard, it is likewise stored only as a hash, and you can
        then also sign in with your e-mail address and password.</li>
      <li><strong>profile data</strong> you enter yourself: display name, handle, bio, profile
        picture or icon with colour, banner, role, status and status text, org name, a Discord tag
        (visible only to you; once Discord is connected, the account fills it in itself, see
        <a href="#discord">Discord</a>), your names on Twitch, YouTube and X, and your
        <a href="#rsi">RSI handle</a>. If you do not enter a display
        name, the account sets a provisional one when it is created: your Discord name or, without
        Discord, the part of your e-mail address before the @. You can change it at any time; it
        only becomes public with a handle (see <a href="#profile">pilot profile</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>uploaded images</strong> (profile picture, banner): your browser crops and
        re-encodes them, so metadata of the original such as camera or location details is not
        uploaded. The image is uploaded as soon as you confirm the crop, even if you do not save
        the profile afterwards. It is kept in a public storage area at Supabase at an address that
        contains your internal account ID, and can be retrieved by anyone; the address saved in
        your profile also shows when you uploaded it.</li>
      <li><strong>favorites</strong> you actively save, including your ship fleet</li>
      <li><strong>tool data</strong> you actively create (crafting planner and “owned” marks,
        refinery jobs, mining presets, hangar loadouts); only you can see it</li>
      <li><strong>friend requests, friendships and messages</strong> (see
        <a href="#friends">friends and messages</a>)</li>
      <li>your <a href="#presence"><strong>online status</strong></a> and the note on what you are
        doing, while you are signed in</li>
      <li>your <a href="#rsi">RSI verification code</a> once you have opened the account dashboard
        (even if you never verify), and after a check also the check result; as a
        <a href="#testpilots">test pilot</a> the related details</li>
      <li>technically, <strong>session and security data</strong>: for each sign-in the IP address,
        browser identifier and times, until you sign out on that device or delete the account; also
        the time the account was created and of the last sign-in. Supabase additionally logs
        requests with IP addresses for a short period set by the provider.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The legal basis is Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR (providing the account feature at your request).
      Signing in on verse-base.com sets no cookies for your session; the session is kept in your
      browser (see <a href="#storage">storage in your browser</a>). Only for administrator accounts
      does the site also set the cookie <code>vb_noanalytics</code> after sign-in, so that this
      browser is not counted (see <a href="#opt-out">opt-out</a>). After signing in with Discord,
      the session also contains the two keys Discord issued for it (an access key and a refresh
      key) until it is first renewed: after about an hour if you have the website open then,
      otherwise on your next visit. The website neither uses nor sends them. If you request a new
      password, Supabase sends an e-mail with a one-time link to that address on our behalf. If you
      change your e-mail address, it sends one e-mail with a one-time confirmation link each to your
      current and to your new address; the change only takes effect once you have confirmed both.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can permanently delete your account yourself at any time in the
      <a href="/account.html">account dashboard</a>. This irreversibly removes: credentials and
      sessions, the profile including online timestamps, favorites, tool data, friendships and
      requests, sent and received messages (also on the other person's side), the Discord link
      including the stored Discord ID, test pilot details and the values copied into the account
      from the Discord server (the bot's own data remains, see
      <a href="#discord-server">Discord server and bot</a>), as well as the copies of your data in
      this browser. <strong>Uploaded profile pictures and
      banners</strong> currently remain as files when you remove them in the dashboard or delete the
      account; send us an e-mail and we will delete them. You remove a verification code in your RSI
      bio yourself on robertsspaceindustries.com.
    </p>
    <p>
      As operator of the database we technically have access to all stored account data, including
      e-mail addresses, sign-in times, the IP addresses of sessions and messages. Within the
      website, administrator accounts additionally see only the
      <a href="#testpilots">test pilot overview</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Processor: Supabase Pte. Ltd., 65 Chulia Street #38-02/03, OCBC Centre, Singapore 049513. The
      database is located in the EU (AWS eu-central-1, Frankfurt); Supabase and its sub-processors
      may, however, also access data from third countries such as Singapore and the USA. This relies
      on the European Commission's standard contractual clauses in Supabase's data processing
      agreement. For details on how the service processes data, see
      <a href="https://supabase.com/privacy" rel="noopener">supabase.com/privacy</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      When you are not signed in, the website only connects to Supabase when you use account
      features (sign-in, password reset) and on three kinds of pages that read public data: a public
      pilot profile (that pilot's profile and favorites when opened, then that pilot's online status
      every 40 seconds while the page is open; uploaded images come from Supabase's storage), the
      Feedback page (the list of credited <a href="#testpilots">test pilots</a>) and the Support
      page (the <a href="#support">donation status</a>; the button there also creates the Stripe
      payment page via Supabase, sending only the amount you chose and the page language). Nothing
      that identifies you is sent; as with any request, Supabase technically learns your IP address.
      The legal basis for these requests is Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR (legitimate interest in showing
      public profiles, the test pilot list and the donation status).
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      While you are signed in, the pages with the site menu connect to Supabase to renew your
      session when needed, read your display name and account role for the menu and write your
      <a href="#presence">online status</a>. Pages with account-bound tools sync their contents and,
      the first time, take over whatever you had saved in this browser while signed out. The account
      dashboard additionally keeps a permanent connection to Supabase through which new messages
      arrive, regularly queries messages, unread messages and your friends' status, and, for a
      verified RSI handle, checks whether your code is still in your RSI bio. On pilot profiles the
      page sends your session along with its requests.
    </p>

    <h2 id="discord">Signing in and connecting with Discord</h2>
    <p>
      Signing in, registering and connecting with Discord are voluntary and only start with your
      click. Your browser then switches to Discord (Discord Inc., 444 De Haro Street, Suite 200,
      San Francisco, CA 94107, USA); after your consent there, Discord transmits to the account
      system your Discord ID, your user name and display name, the address of your profile picture
      and your e-mail address (permissions “identify” and “email”). If no account exists yet, the
      click creates one. If an account with the same confirmed e-mail address already exists, the
      account system links the two automatically.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you create the account with Discord, the profile immediately takes over your Discord
      display name (otherwise the user name), your Discord profile picture and “@username” as Discord
      tag. If you connect Discord later, it takes over picture and name unless you have already set a
      picture and name of your own; it keeps the Discord tag up to date while the connection exists.
      Only you see the Discord tag. The profile picture remains an address on Discord's server
      (cdn.discordapp.com): anyone who sees your profile, your friends list or the chat loads it
      directly from there (see <a href="#images">externally embedded images</a>). The account also
      stores your Discord ID for the <a href="#testpilots">test pilot sync</a> and the values from
      the <a href="#discord-server">Discord server</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you disconnect Discord in the dashboard, this only removes the sign-in connection. The
      details taken over (name, picture address, Discord tag) remain in the account until you change
      them or delete the account. The mapping of your Discord ID also remains and stays in effect:
      our <a href="#discord-server">Discord bot</a> keeps transferring your values from the Discord
      server and whether you hold the “Test Pilots” role into your account, and your access to the
      <a href="#staging">test preview</a> still depends on that; only level, rank and prestige are
      then no longer publicly readable. This ends only when you delete the account or we remove the
      mapping at your request by e-mail. The legal basis is Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR. Discord also
      processes data in the USA and, by its own account, participates in the EU-U.S. Data Privacy
      Framework; Discord's privacy policy applies to signing in with Discord:
      <a href="https://discord.com/privacy" rel="noopener">discord.com/privacy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="profile">Public pilot profile</h2>
    <p>
      Your profile is not public at first. Only when you set a <strong>handle</strong> in the
      dashboard does /pilot/&lt;handle&gt; become visible to anyone, even without signing in: handle,
      display name, bio, profile picture or icon, banner, role, status and status text, org name,
      RSI handle and whether it is verified, where applicable the “Test Pilot” badge, your names on
      Twitch, YouTube and X, since when you have been a member, your
      <a href="#presence">online status</a> and all your favorites including your ship fleet. The
      website's public database interface returns the same details, with the exact time you created
      the account and saved each favorite, and for all profiles with a handle at once. If you are
      linked with Discord and a member of our Discord server, it also returns your level, rank and
      prestige there (see <a href="#discord-server">Discord server</a>); your profile page loads
      these values but does not currently display them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Signed-in users can additionally read the internal account ID of every profile with a handle
      through the interface. If you have uploaded your own profile picture or banner, the ID is also
      part of its address, together with the time of the upload, and can therefore be read by
      anyone (see
      <a href="#accounts">user account</a>). On the website you only find others by their exact
      handle. Not public are your e-mail address, your Discord tag, your verification code, your tool data and hangar
      loadouts and your messages; the website shows your friends list only to you. If you use your Discord picture as profile
      picture, every browser that shows your profile loads the picture directly from Discord; the
      picture address contains your Discord ID, which is therefore publicly readable as well. The
      profile page is blocked for search engines (noindex). If you remove the handle, the profile is
      no longer public. The legal basis is Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR: the public profile is a feature you
      switch on yourself with the handle.
    </p>

    <h2 id="friends">Friends and messages (account only)</h2>
    <p>
      Signed in and with a handle of your own, you can send other pilots a friend request using
      their exact handle. Stored are sender, recipient, status and the times of request and reply,
      and for a friendship both accounts and when it began. Only the two people involved can see
      these details, and the website does not display a friends list publicly anywhere. Declining or withdrawing deletes the request,
      “Remove” deletes the friendship together with all requests between you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Confirmed friends can write each other <strong>messages</strong> in the account dashboard.
      Stored are text, sender, recipient, time and when the message was read. Through the website,
      only sender and recipient can read them; they are, however, not end-to-end encrypted, and we as
      operator of the database and Supabase technically have access. New messages arrive through a
      permanent connection to Supabase while the dashboard is open. Individual messages or
      conversations cannot be deleted; they remain readable for both even after a friendship ends
      and are deleted as soon as either of you deletes their account. The legal basis is
      Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR.
    </p>

    <h2 id="presence">Online status (account only)</h2>
    <p>
      While you are signed in and have a page with the site menu open (all pages except the loadout
      pages of individual ships in the hangar, the one-pager guides and the error page), your browser
      writes timestamps to your profile: when the page was last open and when you last interacted
      with it (mouse, keyboard, scrolling, touch; loading the page and returning to the tab count as
      well). This happens on load, then every 30 seconds, even while the tab is in the background,
      and whenever you return to the tab.
    </p>
    <p>
      On some pages a short note on what you are doing is added, with its time: in the hangar (with
      the selected ship), building a loadout or on a ship page (each with the ship name), planning
      mining or in the refinery; on all other pages it is cleared. Only your confirmed friends see
      this note, and only while it is fresh (3 minutes). It is on until you switch it off in the
      account dashboard (“Friends see what I am doing”); it is then cleared and no longer sent. If a
      browser does not know your setting yet, it only sends the note after learning the setting from
      the account. No other details about which page you are viewing or what you do there are
      transmitted. Nothing is written when you are not signed in.
    </p>
    <p>
      Others do not see the timestamps, only a status derived from them: <em>online</em> (page open
      and used within the last 3 minutes), <em>away</em> (page open but unused for longer, or closed
      less than 3 minutes ago) or <em>offline</em>. This status is only computed once you have set a
      handle. It is then readable by anyone: on your public pilot profile, which refreshes it every
      40 seconds, and through the public interface for all profiles with a handle. To signed-in
      users, the website shows it for friends and open requests. When you sign out in the account
      dashboard, the page first tries to clear the timestamps and the note and then signs you out
      regardless. If clearing fails (for example without a network), this browser writes no further
      timestamps, and the status switches to offline within 3 minutes unless a page of this website
      is still open on another device where you are signed in. Deleting your account removes the
      timestamps together with your profile. As for the rest of the account, the legal basis is
      Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rsi">RSI verification (account only)</h2>
    <p>
      In the dashboard you can voluntarily enter your citizen handle at Roberts Space Industries
      (RSI) and confirm that it belongs to you. For this, the account assigns you a personal code
      (VERSEBASE-VERIFY-…) the first time you open the dashboard and stores it; you put it into your
      public bio on robertsspaceindustries.com. On your click, our server component (a Supabase
      function) fetches the public profile page robertsspaceindustries.com/citizens/&lt;handle&gt;
      and looks for the code there. RSI (Roberts Space Industries International Limited, United
      Kingdom) only learns the handle, and does so from a Supabase server;
      RSI does not learn your IP address, e-mail address or account ID. Once the handle is verified,
      the dashboard repeats the check on every visit; if the code is no longer in the bio, the
      verification is removed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stored are RSI handle, code and check result. If you have set a handle with us, your RSI
      handle (even unverified, as a link to RSI) and the “RSI verified” badge are public on your
      <a href="#profile">pilot profile</a>; only you see the code. If you empty the field and save,
      the RSI handle is deleted; “Revoke verification” only removes the status. The legal basis is
      Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR.
    </p>

    <h2 id="testpilots">Test pilots</h2>
    <p>
      Test pilots try out new features on the <a href="#staging">test preview</a>. Who belongs to
      them follows the “Test Pilots” role on our <a href="#discord-server">Discord server</a>. If
      your Discord ID is mapped to your account, our Discord bot reports to the account system
      whether you hold this role (yes or no), on every change and regularly for reconciliation,
      also after you disconnect Discord in the dashboard (see <a href="#discord">Discord</a>); while
      you hold the role, this keeps your access to the test preview.
      Stored for this are your Discord ID, whether you hold the role, and the time of your last
      visit to the test preview. Administrators see handle, display name, Discord ID and last visit
      of all test pilots in an overview. To revoke someone's access, a block note with a reason can
      be stored.
    </p>
    <p>
      As a test pilot, two things can be public. Both depend on a test-pilot mark in your profile
      that only the account system itself sets, never your browser. If your profile carries it and
      you have a handle, your pilot profile shows the “Test Pilot” badge; only then does the
      dashboard also show the switch “List my name among the Test Pilots”. If you switch it on (off
      by default), your display name (without one, your handle) appears with a link to your
      profile in the Test Pilots list on the <a href="/feedback.html">Feedback</a> page. The legal
      basis is Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR, and for the list your consent (Art. 6 (1) (a) GDPR), which you
      withdraw at any time with the switch. Everything here is deleted with the account.
    </p>

    <h2 id="staging">Test preview (staging.verse-base.com)</h2>
    <p>
      At staging.verse-base.com we run a test preview that is only open to
      <a href="#testpilots">test pilots</a> and administrators. At the entrance you sign in with your
      account; the entrance page connects to Supabase for this. The server then asks the account
      system whether you have access, stores the time of your visit and sets two technically
      necessary cookies: <code>vb_gate</code> (the access pass with your account ID, signed by the
      server, not readable by scripts) and <code>vb_gate_exp</code> (its expiry time), both valid for
      5 minutes. While a page of the preview is visible in the foreground, the browser renews the
      pass about a minute before it expires. While the tab is in the background this pauses; when
      the tab becomes visible again, the browser renews the pass once right away. Each renewal
      updates the visit time. No reach measurement runs on the test preview. The
      legal basis is Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR, and for the cookies Section 25 (2) no. 2 TDDDG. This
      entrance and these cookies do not exist on verse-base.com.
    </p>

    <h2 id="discord-server">Discord server and bot</h2>
    <p>
      VerseBase has a Discord server with its own bot, which we run on our server. For members of
      the Discord server it keeps a level system: per Discord ID it stores experience points (for
      the current run and in total), prestige, the number of your messages, your time in voice
      channels and the time of the last change; it calculates level and rank from these. Each post
      you open in the bug-reports forum earns bonus points once; for this the bot records the post's
      ID, your Discord ID and the time. It does not read the content of messages, but it does write
      the title of such a bug report together with your Discord ID into its operating log on our
      server. All members of the server can see your level, rank, prestige, experience points and
      leaderboard position, via <code>/rank</code>, which works for any member, and via the
      leaderboard; your rank is also shown as a Discord role, and the bot announces level-ups on the
      server. The bot does not show the number of your messages or your voice time there.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bot also reconciles the “Test Pilots” role with website accounts (see
      <a href="#testpilots">test pilots</a>). If your Discord ID is mapped to a website account, it
      transfers your values into that account: level, rank, prestige, experience points, messages,
      voice time, leaderboard position and whether you are currently a member of the server, when
      the bot starts, then every 5 minutes and right after each level-up. This also applies after
      you disconnect Discord, until the mapping is removed (see <a href="#discord">Discord</a>).
      While Discord is connected and you are a member of the server, anyone can read level, rank
      and prestige together with your <a href="#profile">pilot profile</a> through the public
      database interface (the profile page itself does not currently display them); the website
      shows the other values only to you. For the reconciliation the bot sends your Discord ID to
      the account system at Supabase, even if you have no website account: when you leave the
      server, when your “Test Pilots” role changes and when you level up. If the Discord ID is not
      mapped to any account, nothing is stored there.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bot's data is kept in a separate database on our server. We keep it for as long as we run
      the bot; it is not deleted automatically, not even when you leave the server or delete your
      website account. We delete it on request by e-mail. If you have left the server, the
      leaderboard keeps listing your entry, with the first digits of your Discord ID instead of your
      name. The legal basis is
      Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR (legitimate interest in a lively community server). Everything else on the
      server is subject to Discord's privacy policy
      (<a href="https://discord.com/privacy" rel="noopener">discord.com/privacy</a>).
    </p>

    <h2 id="support">Voluntary support (Stripe, PayPal)</h2>
    <p>
      On the <a href="/support.html">Support</a> page you can voluntarily contribute towards the
      running costs of this project. Nothing on this site is behind a paywall, and a contribution
      buys no goods, services or account privileges.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Stripe.</strong> When you press the button, your browser sends only the amount you
      chose and the page language to a server function of this project at Supabase, without any
      details from your account, even when you are signed in. The function asks Stripe to create a
      payment page (amount in euros, fixed product name, page language, return addresses) and sends
      you there. Everything you enter — payment method, card details, billing data, e-mail address —
      you enter <em>on Stripe’s page</em>, not on this one. If you choose PayPal, Apple Pay, Google
      Pay or another method offered there, that provider is involved as well: with PayPal you sign
      in on PayPal’s own page, and with Apple Pay and Google Pay the payment details come from your
      wallet; in each case that provider’s privacy policy applies. Card and bank details never reach
      this website. Stripe processes the payment as an independent controller under its own privacy
      policy. The legal basis for the transfer that starts the process is Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR
      (carrying out the payment you initiated).
    </p>
    <p>
      When you complete the payment, Stripe sends this project's server component a notification
      about it. It contains the details Stripe collected (such as e-mail address, name and
      country). Only a payment that Stripe already reports as paid at that moment (for example by
      card) is stored, and of it only <strong>the amount, the currency, the time, a flag for test or
      live payment and Stripe's reference numbers</strong>. <strong>No name, no e-mail address,
      no address and no payment details are stored in our database</strong>, and only an
      administrator can read the records. Using the reference numbers, a record can be matched to
      the payment in our Stripe account; there, as the recipient, we see what Stripe shows for a
      payment (such as name, e-mail address, payment method and country). We keep the records for
      as long as we keep the donation total. The legal basis for this is Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR
      (legitimate interest in a traceable total).
    </p>
    <p>
      When opened, the Support page reads the <strong>donation status</strong>: the goal, the
      subtotals via Stripe and via PayPal and the time of the last change; the total is displayed.
      This status is readable by anyone. Because the Stripe subtotal and the time change with every
      Stripe payment stored this way, it reveals when the last Stripe donation was made and how much; it
      contains no names. After a completed payment Stripe sends you back with the suffix
      <code>?danke=1</code>, after a cancellation without it. It carries no amount; the page removes
      it right away and the statistics do not count it, while the
      <a href="#hosting">server log</a> records the request like any other.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Payment service provider: Stripe Payments Europe, Ltd. (Ireland), with onward transfer to
      Stripe, Inc. (USA, certified under the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework). For details on how the
      service processes data, see <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy" rel="noopener">stripe.com/privacy</a>.
      A connection to Stripe is only established when you press the button, not when merely reading
      the page.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>PayPal.</strong> As a second route the page carries an ordinary link to PayPal.me. No
      PayPal script runs on this site, and no data is transmitted until you follow it. If you follow
      it, PayPal receives the amount you chose (it is part of the link), your IP address and the
      origin verse-base.com; from then on PayPal's privacy policy applies (PayPal (Europe) S.à r.l. et
      Cie, S.C.A., Luxembourg;
      <a href="https://www.paypal.com/webapps/mpp/ua/privacy-full" rel="noopener">paypal.com</a>). If
      you pay, PayPal shows us as the recipient your PayPal name, your e-mail address and any message.
      We enter the amount by hand into the PayPal subtotal, without names; nothing beyond that is
      recorded on this website. The legal basis is Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR (legitimate interest in
      accepting donations and keeping the total).
    </p>

    <h2>Fonts</h2>
    <p>All web fonts are served locally by this website. No connection to Google Fonts is made.</p>

    <h2 id="third-countries">Recipients outside the EU</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> (USA): every request; certified under the EU-U.S. Data
        Privacy Framework</li>
      <li><strong>Google</strong> (Ireland, with possible transfer to the USA): YouTube after your
        click, our e-mail mailbox; Google LLC is certified under the EU-U.S. Data Privacy
        Framework</li>
      <li><strong>Supabase</strong> (Singapore; access from the USA also possible): account system
        and the Discord bot's reconciliation; standard contractual clauses</li>
      <li><strong>Web3Forms</strong> (India, with service providers also in the USA): feedback;
        standard contractual clauses</li>
      <li><strong>Discord</strong> (USA): signing in with Discord, profile pictures from Discord and
        our <a href="#discord-server">Discord server and bot</a> (level system, rank roles, level-up
        announcements, matching of the “Test Pilots” role); by its own account Discord participates
        in the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework</li>
      <li><strong>Roberts Space Industries</strong> (Roberts Space Industries International
        Limited, United Kingdom, by its own account also with servers in the USA): your RSI handle
        during <a href="#rsi">RSI verification</a> and, while it is verified, on every dashboard
        visit; an EU Commission adequacy decision applies to the United Kingdom</li>
      <li><strong>Stripe</strong> and <strong>PayPal</strong>: donations, according to their own
        privacy policies also in the USA</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      You have the rights under Art. 15–21 GDPR (access, rectification, erasure, restriction, data
      portability, objection) and the right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority
      (Art. 77 GDPR). You can withdraw consent (YouTube click, feedback, Test Pilots list) at any time
      with effect for the future. You can object to processing based on legitimate interests; for the
      reach measurement, the click <a href="#opt-out">above</a> is enough.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Even without a user account we process the data described above (server logs, reach
      measurement, feedback messages, donations and, if you are a member of our Discord server, the
      bot's values); on request we provide access to it or delete it, as far as we can attribute it
      to you. With an account, you view and change your profile data in the dashboard and delete the
      whole account there; what remains is described under <a href="#accounts">user account</a> and
      <a href="#discord-server">Discord server and bot</a>. We keep account data until you change it or delete the
      account. For anything else, such as full access to your data, send us an e-mail.
    </p>
  </div>
